  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nA cold front continues to progress across the forecast area early \nthis morning. Behind the front, winds turn northerly, and will gust \nup to 20 to 30 miles per hour across all but parts of northeastern \nSouth Dakota and western Minnesota this afternoon. Cooler \ntemperatures also take hold behind the front, dropping down to near \nmid-October normals through at least the middle of the week (highs \nin the upper 50s to 60s). Some post-frontal showers will linger \nthrough Monday, with a 40-80% chance to see measurable rainfall \nacross the forecast area, increasing from south to north. Along the \nborder with North Dakota (where model guidance shows a higher degree \nof confidence in multiple rounds of light showers passing through on \nMonday), rainfall totals could reach as high as a tenth to perhaps a \nquarter of an inch at the most. \n\nHigh pressure will take hold of the region for the start of the work \nweek, continuing through Wednesday. With this high pressure \noverhead, winds will progressively decrease, becoming light by the \ntime the high pressure center moves overhead late Tuesday into \nWednesday. Strong humidity recovery and temperatures dropping into \nthe mid-30s Tuesday and Wednesday mornings may bring about chances \nfor frost, particularly on Wednesday. \n\nMid- to late-week, the upper-level low over the western CONUS will \nmove up into the Northern Plains, bringing the next chances for rain \nacross the region. Latest ensemble runs have backed off a bit on the \nrainfall totals over the Aberdeen forecast area, but there is still \na fair degree of spread. Lower end 25th percentiles show only a few \nhundredths, while 75th percentile values fall to around an inch \nthrough Friday morning. This is likely in part due to a somewhat \nsignificant southwesterly shift in the axis of heaviest rainfall \ntotals. Probability to see half an inch of rain now sits at 20-45%, \nincreasing moving from northwest to southeast. This is down from 50-\n70% across the forecast area 24 hours ago, so will continue to keep \nan eye on if this trend downward continues.",
